- **Study 1**: "Frozen shoulder: prospective clinical study with an evaluation of three treatment regimens" — INCLUDE   
(high confidence). Prospective RCT (n=42) randomly allocated to steroid, mobilisations, ice, or no treatment, all taught
pendular exercises; compares manual therapy and exercise.                                                               
- **Study 6**: "Effect of deep transverse friction massage and capsular stretching in idiopathic adhesive capsulitis" — 
INCLUDE (medium confidence). Stated RCT examining deep transverse friction massage (manual therapy) and capsular        
stretching (exercise) in adhesive capsulitis; abstract is minimal but design and topic meet criteria.                   
- **Study 7**: "Comparison of the outcomes of two different exercise programs on frozen shoulder" — INCLUDE (high       
confidence). RCT (n=29) of two exercise programs (glenohumeral ROM vs glenohumeral plus scapulothoracic exercises).     
- **Study 8**: "Mobilization techniques in subjects with frozen shoulder syndrome: randomized multiple-treatment trial" 
— INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT comparing end-range mobilization, mid-range mobilization, and mobilization with        
movement.                                                                                                               
- **Study 9**: "PNF movement patterns compared to the use of conventional free exercises to improve joint ROM in chronic
peri-arthritis of the shoulder" — INCLUDE (low confidence). Compares PNF (exercise approach) to conventional free       
exercises in frozen shoulder; abstract does not explicitly state randomization, so design must be verified at full text.
- **Study 10**: "Comparing effectiveness of antero-posterior and postero-anterior glides on shoulder range of motion in 
adhesive capsulitis—a pilot study" — INCLUDE (high confidence). Pilot RCT randomizing primary adhesive capsulitis to two
mobilization directions.                                                                                                
- **Study 13**: "Intraarticular corticosteroids, supervised physiotherapy, or a combination of the two in the treatment 
of adhesive capsulitis of the shoulder: a placebo-controlled trial" — INCLUDE (high confidence). Placebo-controlled RCT 
(n=93) with four arms including supervised physiotherapy and home exercise.                                             
- **Study 14**: "Efficacy and cost-effectiveness of physiotherapy following glenohumeral joint distension for adhesive  
capsulitis: a randomized trial" — INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT (n=156) randomizing after distension to manual therapy 
and directed exercise vs placebo (sham ultrasound); primary comparison of interest.                                     
- **Study 21**: "Comparison of corticosteroid injections, physiotherapy, and combination therapy in treatment of frozen 
shoulder" — INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT (n=87) comparing corticosteroid, physiotherapy, and combination therapy.     
- **Study 22**: "Comparison of the early response to two methods of rehabilitation in adhesive capsulitis" — INCLUDE    
(high confidence). RCT (n=40) randomizing Cyriax (deep friction massage and mobilisation exercises) vs conventional     
physical therapy.                                                                                                       
- **Study 27**: "The effects of passive joint mobilization on pain and hypomobility associated with adhesive capsulitis 
of the shoulder" — INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT (n=20) of passive mobilization plus active exercises vs active        
exercises alone.                                                                                                        
- **Study 31**: "Comparison of the efficacy of local corticosteroid injections and physiotherapy for the treatment of   
adhesive capsulitis" — INCLUDE (medium confidence). RCT (n=20) randomly divided into injection vs physiotherapy for     
adhesive capsulitis; specific PT content unclear but eligible for conservative screening.                               
- **Study 34**: "Randomised controlled study of Mulligan's vs. Maitland's mobilization technique in adhesive capsulitis 
of shoulder joint" — INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT comparing two manual therapy techniques in adhesive capsulitis.     
- **Study 39**: "Effectiveness of electroacupuncture and interferential electrotherapy in the management of frozen      
shoulder" — INCLUDE (high confidence). Double-blind RCT (n=70) of electroacupuncture plus exercise vs interferential    
plus exercise vs no treatment control.                                                                                  
- **Study 40**: "Effectiveness of physical therapy for patients with adhesive capsulitis: a randomized controlled trial"
— INCLUDE (medium confidence). RCT (n=122) of ibuprofen vs ibuprofen plus combined physical therapy for adhesive        
capsulitis.                                                                                                             
- **Study 41**: "A Comparative study on the efficacy of end range mobilization techniques in treatment of adhesive      
capsulitis of shoulder" — INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT (n=22) comparing exercises and ultrasound vs exercises,        
ultrasound, and end-range mobilization.                                                                                 
- **Study 42**: "The effect of anterior versus posterior glide joint mobilization on external rotation range of motion  
in patients with shoulder adhesive capsulitis" — INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT (n=20) randomizing anterior vs posterior
glide mobilization.                                                                                                     
- **Study 43**: "A randomized controlled trial of intra-articular triamcinolone and/or physiotherapy in shoulder        
capsulitis" — INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT (n=80) with four arms including physiotherapy plus exercise vs placebo.    
- **Study 44**: "Comparison of results of three different methods of treatment for adhesive capsulitis of shoulder" —   
INCLUDE (high confidence). Prospective randomised study (n=72) comparing manipulation under anaesthesia, peri-articular 
injection, and physiotherapy.                                                                                           
- **Study 48**: "A prospective clinical trial comparing chiropractic manipulation and exercise therapy vs. chiropractic 
mobilization and exercise therapy for treatment of patients suffering from adhesive capsulitis/frozen shoulder" —       
INCLUDE (high confidence). Prospective clinical trial with systematic allocation (quasi-randomised) comparing           
manipulation plus exercise vs mobilization plus exercise.                                                               
- **Study 49**: "Comparison of high-grade and low-grade mobilization techniques in the management of adhesive capsulitis
of the shoulder: randomized controlled trial" — INCLUDE (high confidence). RCT (n=100) of high-grade vs low-grade       
mobilization.                                                                                                           
- **Study 50**: "Passive joint mobilisation: does it enhance outcome of adhesive capsulitis of the shoulder following   
corticosteroid injection?" — INCLUDE (medium confidence). Single-blinded pilot RCT (n=14) randomizing home-care vs      
home-care plus passive mobilisation.                                                                                    
                                                                                                                        
**Excluded studies (n = 28):**                                                                                          
- **Study 2**: Observational incidence study in general practice. Not an RCT/quasi-randomised trial.                    
- **Study 3**: Review of textbook principles of joint mobilization. Not a primary clinical trial.                       
- **Study 4**: Open RCT comparing physical therapies alone vs physical therapies plus arthrographic distension and      
steroid. The comparison does not address manual therapy/exercise efficacy versus placebo/no treatment/different manual  
therapy or exercise; it addresses an invasive adjunct.                                                                  
- **Study 5**: Methodological study comparing trial protocols/registry entries with published reports.                  
- **Study 11**: Cross-sectional population survey of upper limb disorder prevalence. Not a treatment trial.             
- **Study 12**: Multiple-subject case report (n=7). Not an RCT.                                                         
- **Study 15**: Observational measurement study evaluating the Shoulder Disability Questionnaire responsiveness.        
- **Study 16**: Non-randomized comparative study with convenience allocation. Not RCT/quasi-randomised.                 
- **Study 17**: Systematic review and cost-effectiveness analysis. Not a primary study.                                 
- **Study 18**: Study comparing anterior vs posterior glide mobilisation; abstract does not indicate randomised or      
quasi-randomised design.                                                                                                
- **Study 19**: Methodological study of outcome reporting bias in Cochrane reviews.                                     
- **Study 20**: Title/abstract mismatch; abstract describes multiple sclerosis cohort, not frozen shoulder.             
- **Study 23**: Prospective observational natural history study. No randomised intervention comparison.                 
- **Study 24**: Methodological paper proposing heterogeneity measures for meta-analysis.                                
- **Study 25**: Methodological chapter on reporting biases.                                                             
- **Study 26**: Prospective observational long-term follow-up. No randomised intervention comparison.                   
- **Study 28**: RCT comparing injections and physical therapy modalities; modalities described as electrotherapy-like,  
not manual therapy or exercise.                                                                                         
- **Study 29**: Laboratory EMG study of muscle activation during exercises, not a treatment efficacy trial.             
- **Study 30**: Comparative study of three approaches for chronic shoulder pain; population not confirmed as adhesive   
capsulitis and design not indicated as randomised.                                                                      
- **Study 32**: Methodological chapter on interpreting results and drawing conclusions.                                 
- **Study 33**: Retrospective study assessing response to treatment. Not RCT/quasi-randomised.                          
- **Study 35**: Methodological chapter on presenting results.                                                           
- **Study 36**: Observational epidemiological study of consultation prevalence.                                         
- **Study 37**: RCT comparing iontophoresis and phonophoresis; interventions are electrotherapy drug-delivery methods,  
not manual therapy or exercise.                                                                                         
- **Study 38**: Prospective randomised trial of painful stiff shoulder (not explicitly adhesive capsulitis) with        
unspecified physiotherapy content.                                                                                      
- **Study 45**: Systematic review of conservative and surgical interventions. Not a primary study.                      
- **Study 46**: Methodological chapter on analysing data and meta-analyses.                                             
- **Study 47**: Prospective investigation of 120 patients; observational analysis of treatment factors rather than a    
randomised comparison of mobilization vs self-exercises.                                                                
                                                                                                                        
### 3. Additional context (if relevant):                                                                                
- The screening yielded **22 included studies out of 50 (44%)**, which is a reasonable yield for this review and avoids 
the red flag of zero inclusions.                                                                                        
- Three included studies (6, 9, 31, 40, 50) are flagged with **medium or low confidence**, primarily because the        
abstract was minimal or randomization was not explicitly described. These should be priority candidates for full-text   
retrieval to confirm design and eligibility.                                                                            
- Study 47 ("Joint mobilization versus self-exercises for limited glenohumeral joint mobility") was excluded despite its
title suggesting an RCT because the abstract describes an observational analysis of treatment factors (frequency of     
sessions and self-exercise compliance) rather than a randomised comparison of mobilization versus self-exercises.       
- Study 4 was excluded because both arms received physical therapy (exercise), so the comparison does not isolate manual
therapy or exercise effects; it tests an invasive adjunct.                                                              
- Several methodological chapters and systematic reviews (3, 5, 17, 19, 24, 25, 32, 35, 45, 46) were correctly excluded 
as non-primary studies.                                                                                                 
